= Solution
{parent=iii}

Fix $C$ and write $m=|A|$. Choose the permitted <prime> $N$ with $Cm<N<2Cm$. Part (ii) gives a <subset> $A'$ of size at least $m/2$ and its <cyclic group> model $B\subseteq\mathbb Z_N$. Thus
$$
\frac{|B|}{N}>\frac1{4C}.
$$
To apply the interval form of <Roth theorem on three-term arithmetic progressions>, represent $B$ in $\{0,\ldots,N-1\}$ and split this interval into two consecutive intervals of length at most $\lceil N/2\rceil$. One has at least $|B|/2\geq m/4$ points. After translation, this is an <integer> <subset> $B_0$ of an interval of length at most $Cm+1$ and <subset density> bounded below in terms of $C$ only. Pad the interval to length $L=\lceil N/2\rceil$ if necessary. If $B_0$ had no nonconstant three-term <arithmetic progression>, part (i) would give
$$
\frac m4\leq|B_0|\leq\frac{C_0L}{\log\log L}\leq\frac{C_0(Cm+1)}{\log\log L}.
$$
Since $L\to\infty$ with $m$, this is impossible once $m$ is sufficiently large depending only on $C$.

Thus $B$ contains distinct elements $b_1,b_2,b_3$ with $b_1+b_3=2b_2$ modulo $N$. Their distinct preimages $a_1,a_2,a_3\in A'$ satisfy $a_1+a_3=2a_2$ in the <integers> by the <Freiman 2-isomorphism>. Reordering the endpoints if necessary gives
$$
\boxed{a_1,a_2,a_3\text{ form a nonconstant three-term arithmetic progression in }A.}
$$
The required size threshold depends on the fixed constant $C$; it is not a claim uniform in an arbitrarily growing $C$.

