Yes. The same telescoping argument proves the <counting lemma for octahedrally quasirandom three-uniform hypergraphs> for every fixed simple three-uniform <hypergraph> $F$.

Label its <vertices of a hypergraph> $1,\ldots,v$ and give <vertex of a hypergraph> $i$ a nonempty host part $V_i$. For each <hypergraph edge> $e=\{i,j,k\}$, let $h_e$ be the corresponding host <hypergraph edge> <indicator function> of <subset density> $p_e$, with $\|h_e-p_e\|_{\square^3}\leq\alpha^{1/8}$. The normalized <labelled hypergraph copy count> is
$$
t_F=\mathbb E_{x_1\in V_1,\ldots,x_v\in V_v}\prod_{e\in E(F)}h_e(x_e).
$$
If the host parts are disjoint, every such <vertex of a hypergraph> choice is automatically <injective>. Enumerate the $m$ <hypergraph edges> as $e_1,\ldots,e_m$ and telescope
$$
\prod_{j=1}^mh_{e_j}-\prod_{j=1}^mp_{e_j}=\sum_{j=1}^m\left(\prod_{i<j}p_{e_i}\right)(h_{e_j}-p_{e_j})\left(\prod_{i>j}h_{e_i}\right).
$$
In the term for $e_j=\{a,b,c\}$, fix all variables outside this triple. Since $F$ is simple and three-uniform, every other <hypergraph edge> intersects $\{a,b,c\}$ in at most two <vertices of a hypergraph>. Its remaining factor therefore depends on at most two of $x_a,x_b,x_c$. Group all two-variable factors according to the pairs $(a,b),(a,c),(b,c)$. Absorb each one-variable factor into any pair containing its variable, and constants into any group. The three resulting pair <functions> remain bounded by one.

The balanced factor is now multiplied by exactly the sort of pair <functions> handled in part (ii). Its average has absolute value at most $\alpha^{1/8}$, independently of the fixed outside variables. Averaging those variables and summing the $m$ errors gives
$$
\boxed{|t_F-\prod_{e\in E(F)}p_e|\leq m\alpha^{1/8}.}
$$
Equivalently,
$$
\boxed{\left|\#F-\left(\prod_ep_e\right)\left(\prod_{i=1}^v|V_i|\right)\right|\leq |E(F)|\alpha^{1/8}\prod_{i=1}^v|V_i|.}
$$
This counts labelled copies preserving the specified <vertex of a hypergraph> classes, for arbitrary intersection patterns among the <hypergraph edges>, and includes the <three-uniform tetrahedron> as a special case.

For a common host <vertex of a hypergraph> <set> of size $n$, the same product average counts homomorphisms. At most $\binom v2n^{v-1}$ assignments identify a pair of distinct labelled <vertices of a hypergraph>, by the <union bound>. Thus restricting to <injective> copies changes the estimate by at most this amount. With a common normalized <subset density> $p$ the <injective> count is therefore $p^mn^v+O(m\alpha^{1/8}n^v+v^2n^{v-1})$. For induced copies, include the non<hypergraph edge> <indicator functions> as additional factors; their balanced <functions> are negatives of the corresponding edge-balanced <functions> and have the same box <norm>. The same proof applies provided all the relevant triple parts satisfy the stated quasirandomness condition.
